00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.389 align:start position:0%
 
hello you're watching the daily climate  show on sky news  on today's programme the uk  is less prepared for climate change than  five years ago  a push to make heating your home more  sustainable and even more affordable  and the refill and reuse campaign hoping  to reduce  millions of tons of plastic waste  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show  where we track the changes happening to  our world right now  and we meet those who are coming up with  the solutions  now the uk has failed to adapt to the  increasing threat of  climate change that is the warning from  a new report by the climate change  commission  it urges the government to act to stop  more people from dying and losing their  homes to extreme weather events  the government says its targets on  reducing co2 emissions are  a world first lisa holland reports from  aberdeen  in this street everybody knows wendy the  flood warden  when the heavy rains come she goes door  to door warning that danger is imminent  morning john how are you today she says  living with the impact of climate change  can be terrifying  it is really frightening because there's  very little that you can do  to mitigate against the river and the  weather  and the tide but at the end of the day  you're still at the mercy of the  elements as it were a 20 million pound  flood defense scheme is being built in  the aberdeenshire town of stonehaven  a place so prone to flooding that the  river charon can burst its banks within  just an hour or so of the alarm being  raised  lilian has had her house flooded twice  before  the older you get the scarier it gets  because you don't  cope with it the same you're trying to  leave your home  if you see the rain you just don't go  out  by 2080 this river will contain a third  more water  because of climate change temperatures  are rising and so are sea levels  but government advisors say the uk is  not prepared  for what lies ahead  and the threats posed by climate change  are getting worse  with criticism that the government's  response is inadequate  lisa holland sky news aberdeenshire well  let's just remind ourselves of the  situation  so far uk land temperatures have risen  by around 1.2 degrees celsius from  pre-industrial  levels and sea levels here have risen  by 16 centimeters since 1900  with an increased risk of flooding  and since 2018 over 4  000 heat related deaths have been  recorded in england that's according to  this assessment  even under the most ambitious emissions  cut scenarios the uk  is likely to experience around an  additional  0.5 degrees celsius increase in annual  average temperature by 2050  but in the last five years since the  publication of the previous report more  than half a million homes have been  built  that are not resilient to those rising  temperatures  well a government spokesperson has  responded to that report  saying the uk was the first major world  economy to set a target of net  zero greenhouse gas emissions by 2050.  our plan to further reduce emissions in  2035 by at least  78 compared to 1990 levels  is the highest reduction target by a  major economy to date  we welcome this report and will consider  its recommendations closely as we  continue to demonstrate  global leadership on climate change  ahead of cop  26 in november well let's speak now to  the chief executive  of the climate change committee which  published the report chris stark  chris good to talk to you a poor report  card  you've given the government there what  are you recommending  we looked at 61 separate risks  also some opportunities and what we've  found is i'm afraid that the uk is not  preparing anywhere near as adequately as  you might expect  that we are for those risks and these  are real issues your report raised some  of them  so we need i think people in this  country need the government to put  better plans in place to manage those  kind of climate risks  and we've done in this report something  i think we haven't been able to do  before  which is to give a much more integrated  sense of the steps that we need to take  not just to tackle  these risks but also to manage net zero  itself which we think will be undermined  if we don't  take these steps to adapt to climate  change we're looking at some pretty  devastating images  on our screen of what happens to  people's lives  when we don't take the necessary  precautions  what is already taking place to mitigate  climate change  so what we've seen for the government  over the last few years has been focused  mostly on things like flooding  now that is a really important hazard  that comes from climate change  we haven't seen them focus on a whole  list of other risks which frankly  we have been raising with them now for  several years things  like overheating in buildings now this  is a really hot day for large suedes of  the country  it's not for me in scotland i should say  but for the southeast  it certainly is and we see overheating  now is that a bigger and bigger claim at  risk  that's something we've been seeing for  more than a decade to the government and  yet  in the last five years since we  published our last response  we've built over half a million homes  that are not  fit for the kind of temperatures that  we'll have in the future they are poorly  adapted to that they don't have shutters  in the windows they're not well  ventilated so they've got their work cut  out for them chris stark  good to talk to you thank you thank you  so  we've heard the warning and the  recommendations well now let's  take a look at our data dashboard  this here this shows the level of human  induced  global warming since the industrial  revolution  just over 1.2 degrees and it's  ticking up the whole time global warming  of more than  one and a half degrees celsius could  potentially expose the world to more  extreme weather  greater economic losses lower crop  yields higher sea levels and  greater damage to wildlife and their  habitats  and this figure here this is the total  amount of global  co2 emissions 2.41  trillion tons and again ticking up  while we watch it well let's take a look  at  some of the day's other climate news now  and companies in australia are being  asked to suggest  offshore locations where carbon dioxide  can potentially  be buried it's part of the australian  government's plan  to help cut emissions the country  already has the world's biggest  commercial carbon capture and storage  project where co2 is being injected  under barrow island that's off western  australia  it contributed to a 5 fall in  australia's carbon emissions  last year a 19th century painting has  inspired plans to develop the landscape  of a national trust property in devon  the painting is believed to be by the  11th baronet sir thomas dyke ackland  whose family gifted the killerton estate  to the trust  nearly 80 years ago the refurbishment  will help it to capture more carbon  and help the land wildlife and livestock  cope with more extreme weather  orcas have complex social structures  including the ability to have close  friendships  a study that used drones to film the  killer whales found that they interact  most with those of the same sex and  similar age within their group of  relatives known as  their pod researchers at the university  of exeter found that as they get older  orcas tend to grow apart there are  believed to be around  50 000 of them left in the wild  now with the weather like it is at the  moment it's hard to imagine isn't it  needing  to heat your home but with climate  change can come  extreme weather and there are now calls  for a  fair heat deal to make moving from  fossil fuel boilers to clean heat pumps  more  affordable now currently heat pump  installation that can  cost as much as 10 000 pounds  the proposed fair heat deal though would  include free heat pumps  and insulation for fuel poor and low  income households and grants for  everyone else  according to the energy saving trust  replacing your old gas boiler with a  standard air heat pump in a four  bedroom detached house could save you  around 375  pounds a year and switching from an old  gas boiler to a heat pump system  could reduce household co2 emissions by  as much as 4  700 kilograms a year that's the  equivalent to taking two cars off the  road  or it's as much carbon as driving from  london to bristol 140 times  well the senior policy advisor at the  independent climate think tank  e3g juliette phillips joins me now  uh juliet good to talk to you it's  important to remember  isn't it that what goes on in our own  homes is just as important as what the  big companies are doing  yes absolutely and with um heating our  homes representing around 14  of the uk's green homes uh greenhouse  gas emissions even on a hot day today  it's still a really important topic to  be discussing  and what else do you think that the fair  heating deal should do  so um the fair heat deal sets out a  number of different um  requests to um mainly treasury focused  on  rebalancing the cost of heating um and  paving the way forward  including things like um covering all  the costs for low-income households  as well as providing attractive  incentives to for those who do have um  support and are able to pay for them  some measures themselves as well as  focusing on the consumer protection side  recognizing  you know it's quite a big change going  into a home and um changing the heating  system so it's really important the high  quality  installations being supported across the  country  julia phillips really good to talk to  you thank you thanks a lot  now have you ever refilled a soap bottle  or filled a container with cereal or  pasta in a store  well if not here is how to join the  refill revolution  [Music]  so  finally even shakespeare has gone  sustainable  a production of as you like it is being  staged with a set  props and costumes made from responsibly  sourced  repurposed and recyclable materials the  play  is on atlee at the watermill theater  that's near newbury in berkshire  and the set designer katie lease says  that the show  has environmentalism at its very core  well that is all for today tomorrow the  british campaign to get  turkey on board with climate action  that's ahead of cop 26 in  glasgow that's at the same time tomorrow  here  on sky news hope to see you then

